            <title>[LU-21244] Administrative write stop and drain for clients (server-side)</title>
                <link>https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-21244</link>
                <project id="10000" key="LU">Lustre</project>
                    <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;ins&gt;&lt;b&gt;Summary&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/ins&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Let an administrator stop a client writes, and drain existing write-back caches to the servers, without unmounting or evicting the clients. Do it by refusing and recalling write locks on a target.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The lock protocol already guarantees that a client may cache writes only under a write lock, and it flushes its dirty pages before it cancels a lock. A server that refuses write locks to a client and recalls the ones the client holds therefore drains that client’s write cache through stock client code paths, with no client change.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;ins&gt;&lt;b&gt;Questions&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/ins&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;Is a server-enforced client write stop and drain acceptable/desirable in Lustre? Would it be a namespace tunable, or a nodemap/export property (e.g. a nodemap &lt;tt&gt;readonly&lt;/tt&gt;-style flag that drains instead of failing writes)? (I lean towards a namespace tunable)&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;Is refusing with &lt;tt&gt;-EINPROGRESS&lt;/tt&gt; the right client-visible behaviour, compared with, say, a dedicated error or a grant-based mechanism?&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;GROUP locks are counted but can’t be recalled. Is there an accepted way for an administrator to break them?&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;Would a generic &lt;tt&gt;lock_counts&lt;/tt&gt; view (per-export lock and callback state) be welcome on its own? It overlaps with the &lt;a href="https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-19157" title="Ban clients from nodemap" class="issue-link" data-issue-key="LU-19157"&gt;&lt;del&gt;LU-19157&lt;/del&gt;&lt;/a&gt; nodemap revoke work in &lt;tt&gt;ldlm_revoke_export_locks()&lt;/tt&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
</description>
